Project Experience

During my internship, I worked on a project called TFLCoMentor.

It is an integrated Learning, Mentoring, Assessment, and Recruitment platform.

The main objective of this platform is to connect Students, Mentors, SMEs, Admins, Employers, and Alumni in a single system.

Each user has a separate dashboard with role-based functionalities.

Let me briefly explain how the platform works.

Admin: Manages users, assigns roles, monitors platform activities, and tracks student progress and employability readiness.

SME (Subject Matter Expert): Creates technical content, question banks, and assessments.

Mentor: Assigns assessments to students, monitors their progress, reviews their performance, provides feedback, and creates learning roadmaps to help them improve their skills.

Student: Learns technical concepts, completes assignments, takes assessments, reviews mentor feedback, follows learning roadmaps, and applies for job opportunities based on company requirements.

Employer: Posts job vacancies, searches for suitable candidates, reviews applications, and conducts interviews to recruit candidates.

Alumni: Former students can also access the platform through their own accounts and manage their profiles.

The platform also includes an AI-powered chatbot that uses mentors' GitHub learning notes as a knowledge source. We use Ollama with the Qwen 3.5 model to generate answers based on the retrieved information.

Apart from MCQ-based assessments, the platform also introduces Video-Based Assessment, where students explain technical concepts through videos, and their understanding and practical implementation can be evaluated.

System Architecture and Modules

Our platform is divided into different modules, and each module handles specific responsibilities.

Skill Taxonomy Module – Java: Manages subjects, concepts, skills, and prerequisites.

Evaluation Content Module – Java: Manages question banks, coding problems, projects, and learning content.

Assessment Module – .NET: Handles assessment creation, scheduling, and student attempts.

Evaluation Module – Node.js: Processes student submissions and automated evaluation workflows.

Insight Module – .NET: Planned for future performance tracking, rankings, and analytics.

Growth Module – Python: Planned for future AI-based learning recommendations and skill improvement tracking.

Membership and Roles Module – Node.js: Handles user authentication, authorization, role management, and user profiles.

